      "method": "item/started",
      "params": {
        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"2. Show that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(m, n)$ such that\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}\\n$$\\nis a positive integer.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"british_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"For k≥0, define integers t_k,y_k by\\n\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\n\\nt_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,  y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\nA direct calculation gives\\n\\nt_{k+1}^2-3y_{k+1}^2=t_k^2-3y_k^2.\\n\\nSince t_0^2-3y_0^2=-2, induction yields\\n\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2                                                   (1)\\n\\nfor every k≥0. Also, t_k and y_k are positive odd integers: this holds initially and is preserved by the recurrence. Moreover, y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k, so the y_k are all distinct.\\n\\nSet\\n\\nn_k=(y_k+1)/2,\\n\\nwhich is a positive integer. From (1),\\n\\nt_k^2=3(2n_k-1)^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\\n\\nThus t_k^2 is the discriminant of\\n\\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+(n_k^2+n_k)=0.                                    (2)\\n\\nBecause t_k and 4n_k-1 are both odd,\\n\\nm_k=(4n_k-1+t_k)/2\\n\\nis a positive integer. By the quadratic formula it satisfies (2), and hence\\n\\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k=4m_kn_k.\\n\\nDividing by the positive integer m_kn_k gives\\n\\n(m_k+1)/n_k+(n_k+1)/m_k=4,\\n\\nwhich is a positive integer. Finally, the n_k are strictly increasing because the y_k are, so the pairs (m_k,n_k) are all distinct. Therefore there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers (m,n) with the required property.\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a094c0-23ee-7bd0-88fe-740b0db4aa6b",
          "type": "userMessage"
